I'm with you here. The wooden staff fits the wizard image much better
than throwing daggers. I noticed that many (mostly newbie-ish) players
tend to wish for the staff of misspelling when playing a wizard, which
seems to confirm this. Nowadays, I sell my staff to the next shopkeeper
as soon as I get even an uncursed orcish dagger to practice with,
although that little part of me that kept the roleplaying attitude to
the game weeps at that.

The real problem is, wizards are absolutely not cut for melee fighting,
so an early wizard needs a backup ranged weapon for when his attack
spells are inappropriate or unavailable (FB against nymphs, low mana,
...). Another artifact staff wouldn't help much to compensate for the
weaknesses of early wizards, and late game wizards really wouldn't need
it anymore. 

Maybe you could just convert the wizard quest artifact to a staff, just 
to fit the image better. Keep the same special properties, there's really 
no need for an amulet of ESP usually, and if you made some properties
dependant on wielding the thing, there'd be a nice tradeoff between
wielding the staff and wielding magicbane, both of which you wouldn't
really use as a weapon anyway.

